I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, diffraction pattern, and the provided tungstate workflow/tooling first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, refine them in GSAS-II, validate the exported profiles/CIFs against the final project, and document any ambiguity.

The provided workflow requires an environment check before refinement. I’ve confirmed this is a SrCO₃ + WO₃ sample fired at 900 °C with raw Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ reflection data; I’m running that check now, then I’ll begin peak and phase analysis.

That check failed only because the separate automated-research workshop/controller is intentionally undeployed (`deployment_complete: false`). The user explicitly excluded that research process, so it does not prevent this direct sample analysis; the local GSAS-II and crystallographic resources are still in scope. I’m proceeding with those tools and will keep all work in the requested workspace.

The pattern is dense enough that a chemistry-only guess would be unsafe. I’m now comparing its peak positions against the complete frozen COD subsets for Sr–W–O, Sr–C–O, W–O, and Sr–O, including alternate polymorphs and possible residual precursors.

Initial matching confirms scheelite SrWO₄ as the dominant phase and a tungsten-oxide-like triplet at 23.46/23.97/24.73°. Several remaining peaks cannot be explained by those two alone, so I’m testing residual SrO/SrCO₃ and Sr-rich tungstate analogues rather than forcing a two-phase refinement.

I’m moving from line matching to controlled whole-pattern tests. The first GSAS-II comparison will quantify how much SrWO₄ + WO₃ explains; subsequent models will add one candidate phase at a time, with identical background/instrument treatment, so improvements can be attributed to phase content rather than extra nuisance parameters.

The first automated refinement sweep exposed a numerical issue rather than a scientific conclusion: weak candidate scales were collapsing to zero, after which unconstrained cell refinement became singular. I’m tightening the staging—first fitting only background/scales, then adapting candidate cells from indexed peaks before allowing cell refinement. This preserves useful rejection evidence while avoiding false failures from unstable least squares.

The residual search is already finding two strong, distinct framework matches: a monoclinic SrWO₄ polymorph and Sr₃W₂O₉ both outperform tungsten oxides on unexplained peaks. I’m letting the full direct/analogue library ranking finish before choosing which structural hypotheses deserve stable refinements.

The adapted monoclinic models now refine with nonzero abundance, confirming that phase family is real in the profile. Their unconstrained monoclinic metric wandered into an equivalent-looking but physically unjustified cell setting, so I’m adding tight reciprocal-cell bounds around the indexed starting cells before accepting any structure or fraction.

The bounded two-phase refinements have completed cleanly; the three-phase refinements are slower because the low-symmetry WO₃ phase generates hundreds of reflections. I’m continuing them, then I’ll compare Rwp, physically allowed profile parameters, and residual peak coverage—not Rwp alone.

The remaining peaks show a coherent cubic Sr₃WO₆-type fingerprint: the adapted Pa-3 analogue predicts the otherwise unexplained lines near 16.62, 29.76/30.90, 32.63, 39.04, 44.62, 52.01, 54.13, 57.0, 59.57, 61.22, 62.73, and 67.76°. I’m now testing whether that phase replaces the tentative monoclinic SrWO₄ assignment or is required alongside it.

The phase inventory is now well constrained: scheelite SrWO₄ is dominant, with monoclinic SrWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and cubic Sr₃WO₆ accounting for the remaining crystalline signal. I’m refining structural and line-shape details next, then I’ll export and replay-check the exact GSAS-II project so the CIFs, fractions, and profile all come from one consistent model.

The four-phase model reduces the profile residual substantially and gives a chemically balanced Sr:W inventory, but its current peak-shape parameters are pinned at bounds. I’m separating structural-coordinate tests from profile terms now; this prevents a broadening parameter from masquerading as a different phase or distorted structure.

A useful diagnostic surfaced: GSAS-II clips out-of-range parameters only after a refinement cycle, so the earlier 12.04% figure was attached to a pre-clipping profile and was not replay-stable. I’m correcting for that explicitly—every candidate now gets a second calculation/refinement after bound handling, and only replayed residuals will be reported. This is also why I’m treating the minor-phase atomic coordinates conservatively.

The remaining mismatch is mostly explainable by physical specimen effects rather than an extra strong phase: the four fractions give an Sr:W ratio essentially equal to the 1:1 precursor ratio, and the unexplained peaks index onto the minor phases. I’m now searching the specimen-height/zero combination and texture/anisotropic broadening in small, replay-stable steps; these are exactly the effects the metadata says were uncalibrated.

I’ve narrowed the pattern to four crystalline phases and am now finishing the line-shape refinement and replay checks. The main remaining concern is ensuring the saved GSAS-II project reproduces the submitted profile exactly, especially after parameter bounds are applied.

The bounded surface-roughness correction is stable and lowers the fit to Rwp 11.41%. I’m now tightening the unit cells and checking whether the remaining mismatch is structural (atomic coordinates/thermal factors) or chiefly unresolved peak overlap, before freezing the final model.

The cell re-refinement is behaving well: the scheelite metric barely moves, while the two monoclinic minority cells shift only slightly and improve Rwp to 11.39%. That stability supports the phase assignments rather than a peak-position fit being forced by large lattice distortions.

The dominant scheelite oxygen coordinates refine reproducibly, but the unconstrained least-squares displacement is a little larger than the generator’s physically allowed perturbation. I’m treating that as a warning against overinterpreting the light-atom positions and am profiling the per-phase displacement factors separately before deciding what to retain.

The phase-isotropic displacement factors are identifiable only weakly for the minor phases, but the profile minima are internally consistent: about 0.012 Å² for scheelite, near the allowed upper end for monoclinic SrWO₄, and about 0.009 Å² for WO₃. Heavy-atom coordinate shifts are being retained only within the known perturbation envelope.

The constrained coordinate fits produce small but repeatable improvements without changing any phase identity or cell appreciably. Several light-atom coordinates land on the allowed perturbation envelope, so those positions will be reported as low-confidence refinements rather than precise structure determinations.

The physically bounded instrument refinement converges at U = 0, W = 40, X = 0.1, SH/L ≈ 0.0251 and polarization ≈ 0.761, with a stable Rwp of 11.12%. I’m now freezing all non-linear terms, performing a clean replay-style recalculation, and screening the exported structures for impossible contacts before writing the deliverables.

The four-phase model is now stable and physically screened. I’m generating the requested deliverables from that exact saved refinement, then I’ll replay `final.gpx` once more to verify the CIFs, fractions, and profile are reproducible.

The exact replay of the submitted project passed: it regenerated all four phase patterns from the saved atoms with no profile-review flags, preserved the project byte-for-byte, and reproduced the reported phase fractions. I’m checking export consistency and documenting the model limits now.

Completed the four-phase GSAS-II refinement.

| Phase | Crystalline weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| SrWO₄, scheelite I41/a | 63.18% |
| SrWO₄, monoclinic C2/c | 10.30% |
| WO₃, monoclinic P21/c | 10.97% |
| Sr₃WO₆, cubic Pa-3 | 15.55% |

Final fit: Rwp 11.1189%, Rp 7.6412%, GOF 3.1485. An independent replay of `final.gpx` reproduced the profile and fractions exactly. Fractions exclude amorphous material; the minor-phase fractions and detailed oxygen coordinates carry additional systematic uncertainty.

